{
  /* The browser client. Bundler resolution and the DOM libs, because vite is
     what builds this and a browser is what runs it.

     "exclude" is stated explicitly, and must stay stated: the base config
     deliberately carries no file list, but if one ever creeps back in it would
     be inherited here and could silently empty this project. `npm run
     typecheck` fails if any project checks zero files, which is the real
     guard — see scripts/typecheck.ts. */
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "Bundler",
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "types": ["vite/client"],

    /* The `@/` alias shadcn generates its imports against. It lives HERE and
       not in tsconfig.base.json, because `baseUrl` resolves relative to the
       config that declares it: in the base it would point at the repo root
       for tests/ and the node side too, and `@/lib/utils` would resolve to
       a file that does not exist there. Mirrored in vite.config.ts and
       vitest.config.ts — three resolvers, three separate failure modes.
       No `baseUrl`: TypeScript 7 removed it (TS5102), and `paths` entries now
       resolve relative to this file, which is what we want anyway.
       See docs/project/typechecking.md. */
    "paths": { "@/*": ["./*"] }
  },
  /* **`../*.d.ts` was here and is not any more**, which is worth a note because
     it will look like an omission to anyone who reads tests/tsconfig.json, where
     the same glob is still needed.

     It was added on 2026-09-03 for one import: `useStepJob.ts` took a type-only
     `StepBefore` from `src/pipeline.ts`, and although nothing of that reached
     the browser, tsc still walked the module for its types — through the
     pipeline to `src/fetch.ts` and `src/pdf.ts`, whose untyped dependencies are
     declared in `src/*.d.ts`. Without the glob those resolved as `any` and the
     project failed TS7016 on packages the client never loads. The cost was the
     pipeline's whole type closure being checked here as well as by the root
     project: 267 files to 346, and 2.5s → 3.2s across `npm run typecheck`.

     That import moved to `src/step-order.ts` on 2026-09-04. There is still a
     closure — `step-order.ts` reaches `types.ts`, and that reaches `messages.ts`,
     `assets.ts` and `ids.ts` — but every one of those was already in this
     project, and none of them touches an untyped package, so the glob has
     nothing left to declare.

     **What stops the glob being needed again is tests/client-imports.test.ts,
     not TS7016.** GPT Sol's correction, 2026-09-04: a future client import of a
     server module would only fail here if that module happened to reach an
     untyped package, and a fully typed one would typecheck in silence. The
     layering rule is the diagnostic; this `include` was only ever the plaster
     over one breach of it. The fix, then and next time, is to move the shared
     piece into a module that imports nothing. */
  "include": [".", "../types.ts"],
  "exclude": []
}
